  "rationale": "The requirement has one obligation. The `habeas-bootstrap-requirements` skill must direct agents to propose a distributed requirement for a cross-cutting convention, instead of marking each site `{impl}`.\n\n`tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[bootstrap_skill_proposes_distributed_requirements_for_conventions]` is marked `{test}` for this requirement. It does three things:\n- It loads the bundled `habeas-bootstrap-requirements` skill's `SKILL.md` with `skill_md`.\n- It extracts the `## Proposing requirements` section with `section`. That helper takes the body up to the next heading of the same or a higher level and skips headings inside code fences.\n- It uses `assert_text` to assert that the section contains the phrase \"propose a distributed requirement instead of marking each site `{impl}`\".\n\nI checked this against `assets/skills/habeas-bootstrap-requirements/SKILL.md`. Its `Proposing requirements` section says: \"When a convention applies on its own at many sites, such as how every validation function continues after a failed check, propose a distributed requirement instead of marking each site `{impl}`\". The sentence is wrapped across lines in the source, and `section` returns a collapsed body, so the test's single-line phrase matches. Removing or rewording the directive in that section would fail the test. The section-scoped assertion also keeps the guidance tied to the proposing step, not to an unrelated part of the skill.\n\nA second test, `fn[bundled_skills_say_when_to_read_each_reference_file]`, pins the full sentence, including the pointer to `references/distributed-requirements.md`. It reinforces the check but isn't needed for adequacy.\n\nThe text names no other modes, boundaries or failure cases. It is a documentation-content requirement, and the assertion enforces the directive's wording.",
